  "landfallEstimate": "To cross phase 4 during 16th to 21st November",
  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTROPICAL WEATHER OUTLOOK\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 14.11.2026\nTROPICAL WEATHER OUTLOOK FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN (THE BAY OF\nBENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168 HOURS ISSUED AT 0800\nUTC OF 14.11.2026 BASED ON 0600 UTC OF 14.11.2026.\nBAY OF BENGAL:\nYesterday\u2019s low pressure area over Southeast Bay of Bengal lay as a well marked low pressure\narea over the same region at 0000 UTC and persisted over the same region at 0300 UTC of\ntoday, the 14th November, 2026.\nIt is very likely to move northwestwards and intensify into a depression over the same region during next\n24 Hours. Thereafter, it is likely to move north-northwestwards and intensify further into a cyclonic storm\nover the central parts of south and adjoining westcentral Bay of Bengal around 1200 UTC of 16th\nNovember.\nEstimated central pressure is around 1009 hPa. Associated maximum sustained wind speed is\n15 knots gusting to 25 knots.\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained surface Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. 0E) wind speed (Kmph) disturbance\n14.11.26/0300 7.3/87.8 35-45 gusting to 55 Well Marked Low Pressure Area\n14.11.26/1200 7.6/87.2 35-45 gusting to 55 Well Marked Low Pressure Area\n15.11.26/0000 8.8/86.7 40-50 gusting to 60 Depression\n15.11.26/1200 10.0/86.6 45-55 gusting to 65 Depression\n16.11.26/0000 10.8/86.5 50-60 gusting to 65 Deep Depression\n16.11.26/1200 11.5/86.4 60-70 gusting to 80 Cyclonic Storm\n17.11.26/0000 12.0 /86.3 65-75 gusting to 85 Cyclonic Storm\n17.11.26/1200 12.6/86.1 70-80 gusting to 90 Cyclonic Storm\n18.11.26/0000 13.2/85.9 75-85 gusting to 95 Severe Cyclonic Storm\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex lay over the Southeast Bay of Bengal and its neighbourhood centered\nwithin 6.8N/87.8E. Intensity of the system is characterized as T1.0. Associated scattered to\nbroken low and medium-level clouds, with embedded intense to very intense convection lay over\nSouth Bay of Bengal, South Andaman Sea, Andaman & Nicobar Islands area and Sri Lanka. The\nminimum cloud-top temperature (CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -90\u00b0C. The multi-satellite winds\nindicate stronger winds in southeast sector.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak: Cloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-\n25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very Intense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of\ndepression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThis is a guidance Bulletin for WMO/ESCAP Panel Member countries. Visit respective National websites for Country specific Bulletins.\nRemarks:\nMadden Julian Oscillation (MJO) is in phase 3 with amplitude more than 1. It is likely to continue\nin same phase till 15th and then move across phase 4 during 16th to 21st November. Thus, MJO is\nfavorable for enhancement of convective activity and cyclogenesis over Bay of Bengal during\n14th to 21st November.\nGuidance from NCICS model indicates prevalence from westerly wind anomaly (>9mps) over\nsouth BoB during 14th to 17th along with prevalence of Equatorial Rossby Wave, Kelvin Wave\nand MJO with easterly wind anomaly (5-7mps) to its north. Thus, equatorial waves are phase\nlocked and would support cyclogenesis over the south BoB during next 5 days.\nLow level positive vorticity is elliptical, and is southeast-northwest oriented with value around 50 X10-6 s-1\nto the south of system centre. The upper level divergence is around 30X10-6 s-1 to the southwest of\nsystem area. It is oriented northwest to southeast. Low level convergence is around 30 X10-6 s-1 to the\nsouthwest of system centre and is southwest-northeast oriented. IR-WV imagery indicates intense\nconvection to the northwest of system centre (-3 to -40C). The upper tropospheric ridge runs along 12\u00b0N in\nassociation with anticyclonic circulation over north Andaman S",
